'''''Hirschfeldia incana''''' (formerly ''Brassica geniculata'') is a species of flowering plant in the [[Brassicaceae|mustard family]] known by many common names, including '''shortpod mustard''', '''buchanweed''', '''hoary mustard'''<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |accessdate=2014-10-17 }}</ref> and '''Mediterranean mustard'''.<ref name=grin>{{GRIN | accessdate = 12 January 2018}}</ref> It is the only species in the monotypic genus '''''Hirschfeldia''''', which is closely related to ''[[Brassica]]''.<ref name=warwick>Warwick, S., ''et al.'' [http://www.docstoc.com/docs/7240370/Guide-to-Wild-Germplasm-of-Brassica-and-wbr-Allied Guide to Wild Germplasm of ''Brassica'' and Allied Crops (tribe Brassiceae, Brassicaceae). 2nd Edition.] Agriculture & Agri-Food Canada.</ref> The species is native to the [[Mediterranean Basin]] but it can be found in many parts of the world as an [[introduced species]] and often a very abundant [[noxious weed]].<ref name=pier>[http://www.hear.org/pier/species/hirschfeldia_incana.htm PIER Weeds of the Pacific].</ref> This mustard is very similar in appearance to [[Brassica nigra|black mustard]], but is generally shorter.<ref name=tchester>[http://tchester.org/plants/analysis/mustard/comparison.html Identification: ''B. nigra'' v. ''H. incana'']</ref> It forms a wide basal rosette of lobed leaves which lie flat on the ground, and it keeps its leaves while flowering.<ref name=jepson>[http://ucjeps.berkeley.edu/cgi-bin/get_JM_treatment.pl?2240,2496,2497 ''Hirschfeldia incana'']. [[Jepson Manual]] Treatment.</ref> Its stem and foliage have soft white hairs. Unlike black mustard, ''H.&nbsp;incana'' is a perennial plant.<ref name=tchester/>

Its leaves are edible and traditionally were used in some areas as a [[List of leaf vegetables|leaf vegetable]].<ref>[http://www.pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Hirschfeldia+incana PFAF ''Hirschfeldia incana'']</ref>

==Subspecies==
Four subspecies are accepted.<ref name = powo/>
*''Hirschfeldia incana'' subsp. ''consobrina'' {{small|(Pomel) Maire}}
*''Hirschfeldia incana'' subsp. ''geniculata'' {{small|(Desf.) Tzvelev}}
*''Hirschfeldia incana'' subsp. ''incana''
*''Hirschfeldia incana'' subsp. ''incrassata'' {{small|(Thell.) Gómez-Campo}}

